description:
Found by the s525q binder dual-run, perl-tests/local.t:652/660: _binding_at answers undef (not lexical) for `$FETCH`, the binder answers `my $FETCH` declared at 649:20.

Cause (read, Pl/Parser2.pm): `_stmt_binding` -> `_stmt_declares_canon` / `_declared_names` -> `_declarator_syms($stmt)` returns only the symbol(s) right after the statement's FIRST keyword (`$k[1]`); a second `my` later in the same statement's initializer is a declaration too (perl: pad_add_name_pvn for each `my` op; both are introduced at the next statement by intro_my).

Probe (scratch/s525q/probes/B4.pl in the s525q worktree):

    sub run {
        my $S = my $F = 0;
        package T;
        sub g { ++$F; ++$S }
        package main;
        T::g(); T::g();
        print "F=$F S=$S T::F=", $T::F // 'u', "\n";
    }
    run();

perl 5.40.3:  F=2 S=2 T::F=u
base (main 81ef05e5) ./runpcl:  F= S=2 T::F=2   -- SILENT WRONG (the named sub's ++$F writes $T::F; the lexical $F stays undef and prints empty)
s525q tree: identical to base.  The same shape in a file-level bare block (B3.pl) gives the same wrong answer.
$S (the first declarator) is right; only the chained one is lost.

Fix belongs to 1c (requalifier port: the binder already records every `my` of the statement).  A legacy fix would make _declarator_syms (or a new caller-side walk) collect every `my/our/state` declarator Symbol of the statement -- check the other _declarator_syms callers (_declared_names is used by the forward-declaration and our-alias paths) before widening it.  NOT fixed in s525q (inert batch).
